p.src:
This is the point at which Rawls moves beyond meritocracy. The lecture is especially careful here not to collapse into caricature. Rawls does not say that the gifted must be handicapped or that equality requires leveling all outcomes. Rather, he shifts the question from whether talents may be exercised to the terms on which their fruits may be held: $\text{no leveling equality}
\qquad
\text{but}
\qquad
\text{change the terms on which talent-based gains are held}.$

